RTM Test Harness
================

The harness is a small pytest suite that passively listens to the rig's UDP
traffic on the host PC. There is no firmware actuation surface — RUN/RESET
must be driven from the front panel, and power is wall-switched.

Layout
------
- ``test/rig/parser.py``       — wire frame → typed dataclass
- ``test/rig/monitor.py``      — UDP listener thread + frame queue
- ``test/rig/assertions.py``   — ``wait_for`` / ``expect`` helpers
- ``test/scenarios/``          — pytest scenarios (one file per S-numbered case)
- ``test/log/<YYYY>/<MM>/<DD>/<HHMMSS>_pytest.log`` — per-session log

Network setup
-------------
- ClearCore: ``10.0.0.10``
- XPB:       ``10.0.0.11``
- Host PC:   ``10.0.0.100``  (must be set on the interface bound to the rig)
- Production UDP port:  ``8888``
- Observer UDP port:    ``8889``

The monitor sends a lightweight observer beacon once per second to CC and XPB
on port 8888. While the beacon is fresh, both boards tee directed-broadcast
debug copies on port 8889, so a single UDP bind on the PC sees both directions
through any unmanaged switch. If the PC or harness is absent, the boards use
only the production CC↔XPB unicast path.

Running
-------
From repo root:

::

    python -m pytest test/scenarios/ -v

  Use ``--rig-port`` to override the observer capture port and
  ``--rig-firmware-port`` to override the board control/beacon port.

Markers:

- ``live_rig`` — requires the rig to be powered and on the network.
- ``manual``   — requires operator action (e.g. press RUN/RESET).
- ``slow``     — long-running scenarios.

Skip the manual ones with ``-m "not manual"``.

Conventions
-----------
- Each test gets a fresh ``Monitor`` (function scope) — frame buffer and clock
  start at zero per test.
- Pass/fail is determined from observable UDP telemetry (``HB;STATE=…``,
  ``STAT;…``, ``NOTICE;…``, ``PR_*``) — never from internal firmware state.
- On failure, dump the last N frames so the log file is enough to diagnose.
